                    %br/
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D6+N%3D%282%2C0%29+SCFT"} D=6 N=(2,0) SCFT
                    %br/
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D6+N%3D%281%2C0%29+SCFT"} D=6 N=(1,0) SCFT
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "supersymmetry#Shnider88"}> Shnider 88
              , also
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "supersymmetry#Nahm78"} Nahm 78
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "supersymmetry#Minwalla98"} Minwalla 98, section 4.2
          %h2#properties_of_4d_sym Properties of 4d SYM
          %h3#conformal_invariance Conformal invariance
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=4
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D=4
            SYM is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SCFT"} SCFT
          %h3#ClosedExpressionsForPhysicalObservables Closed expressions for physical observables
          %p
            Among all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangians"} Lagrangians
            that of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo =
                    %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=4
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = 4
            SYM is special in several ways, in particular of course in that it is conformally invariant and in that it has maximal
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} KK-reduction
            of the very special
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
            and related by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT"} AdS7-CFT6 duality
            to the very special theory of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
          %p
            Accordingly, it is to be expected that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo =
                    %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=4
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = 4
            SYM satisfy special relations that make them more tractable than the observables of a generic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/planar+limit"} planar limit
            of the theory, where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            are dominated by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            that can be given the structure of planar
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphs"} graphs
          %p This includes notably the following phenomena:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+spectrum"} operator spectrum
                of the dilatation operator (the part of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stress-energy+tensor"} stress-energy tensor
                which induces conformal transformations) can be expressed in closed form, indeed when regarded as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
                it defines an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrable+system"} integrable system
                equivalent to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+chain"} spin chain
                models. This has been used in particular to explicitly check aspects of the conjectured
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+duality"} AdS-CFT duality
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                        %mo =
                        %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=4
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo =
                      %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D= 4
                SYM with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
                on
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetimes"} anti de Sitter spacetimes
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#Beisert"} Beisert et al
            %li
              %p
                Certain
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
                called
                %em maximally helicity violating amplitudes
                (“
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MHV+amplitudes"}> MHV amplitudes
                ”) simplify drastically as compared to the generic situation and in fact are controled by a certain
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twistor+string+theory"} twistor string theory
                whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twistor+space"} twistor space
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Monteiro"} Monteiro
            %li
              %p
                Generally, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
                of the theory in the planar limit have certain closed-form combinatorial expressions. See (
                %a{:href => "#Arkani-Hamed"}> Arkani-Hamed et al
                ).
          %p
            Such “exact solutions” of the theory are of interest in that even though N=4, D=4 SYM is very different from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenologically viable
            models such that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            in that it is highly (super-)symmetric and conformal, it is still similar enough (being a nonabelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+coupling"} minimally coupled
            to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
          %p
            In other words, because understanding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            in general is difficult, going to special points in the space of all such theories – such as the point of N=4, D=4 SYM – may be hoped to yield a tractable approximation. For more on this way of studying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            and other realistic theories by studying instead their highly symmetric but phenomenologically unrealistic siblings, see also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+results+applied+elsewhere"} string theory results applied elsewhere
          %h3#holography_adscft Holography, AdS-CFT
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=4
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d=4
            SYM is supposed to be related under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+superstring+theory"} type II superstring theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} compactified
            on a 5-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
            to an asymptotically
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} anti de Sitter spacetime
          %div
            %p
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
                induced via
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+correspondence"} AdS-CFT correspondence
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
                      perspective via
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS7-CFT6"} AdS7-CFT6
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
                    perspective
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11d+supergravity"}> 11d supergravity
                    \/
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
